WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:05.165
We went with a lookalike
because there are actual replicas.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:10.970
People have built very close-to-scale
replicas of a 1911, 1910 Wright Flyer,

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.872
but they found the same problems
the Wrights did.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:15.542
The airplane isn't exactly
what you call reliable.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.277
Very often, it crashed.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.013
And frankly, we don't want to have
to fix our airplane after every flight.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:21.347
We don't like the “crash” word.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:25.718
So we built out of modern materials
with modern building techniques,

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.221
so that we could fly on a frequent basis.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.858
We'd like to be able to land the airplane
and put somebody else on it

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.393
and continue flying.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:46.139
The airplane is very, very close to what
a 1911 Wright B Flyer would have looked like.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:51.111
Once you get 100, 200 feet away from it,
if we overfly people,

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:52.045
a 1911 Wright Flyer is what you see.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:56.049
We're trying to recreate
what people in 1911 Dayton, Ohio,

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:59.619
would have seen if Orville or Wilbur
had flown over top of them.

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:02.722
And when you're inside the airplane,
this is not an open cockpit airplane.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.557
This is a no cockpit airplane.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:08.428
You are literally sitting in front of
the wing, and experiencing flight

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:11.097
the way that people did
at the early turn of the century.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:34.954
The Wrights knew that in order to roll the airplane,
they had to change the lift of each wing.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.956
They literally just twisted the wings.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.559
The wood would bend inside the airplane.

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:43.963
They had a small tray that they sat on,
and when they shifted their body,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.299
there were wires that were hooked
to the ends of the wing,

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:50.069
and that would twist each wing
in opposite directions

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:52.005
to be able to give them
that lift differential.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.874
The replicas that have flown
have done that.

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:57.577
It's incredibly difficult
to be able to control.

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.812
So to be able to get something
where you can

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:04.184
safely and repeatedly do that
is very, very, very challenging.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.553
And that's why we went with the ailerons.

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:25.838
The Wrights actually started out
as bicycle engineers, if you will.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:26.973
They were bicycle mechanics.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.275
So they were very familiar with chains.

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:31.244
And when they realized
they could only have one engine,

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:33.079
but they needed two propellers to generate

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:35.148
the thrust that they needed
to overcome the weight,

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:39.285
they very quickly realized a single chain
going to each propeller,

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:42.388
one of them is inverted so that
you have counter-rotating propellers,

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:42.956
was the way to do it.

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:45.458
So we've done the same thing
on our airplane.

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:48.761
We have one engine, and we have
two propellers that are driven

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:52.699
by, frankly, a very, very,
very strong and very industrial chain.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.768
Yes, it looks like a bicycle chain,
but when you get up close to it,

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:59.439
we've got a pretty solid chain
on this airplane for very good reasons.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:03.843
We have a Lycoming IO390,
probably the largest four cylinder

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.247
general-aviation engine
that we could get to put on the airplane.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.849
So rated at 210 horsepower.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:14.020
The Wrights had anywhere
from 32 to around 40 horsepower,

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.588
but sometimes you got a little less,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.591
sometimes you got a little more
because they were all hand-built.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:41.080
It actually flies much like any other conventional
general-aviation airplane,

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:44.617
except for the fact that you're not sitting
inside of the airplane when you fly.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:48.454
You and I are both part
of the drag equation of this airplane.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:52.325
You are impacting the drag of the airplane
when you stick your hand out and wave.

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:53.793
She's got a personality to her.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:54.861
And that's why we're required

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:57.897
to go through the flight test process,
because the FAA knows

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.966
the airplane is going to
have a personality.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:03.636
The FAA has a very rigorous process
when you're building even

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.671
an experimental airplane.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:10.576
We loaded the wings down with salt bags
to simulate the load of 3½ Gs.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.546
So the airplane, we know can pull 3½ Gs.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:15.982
You're never going to see 3½ Gs
in this airplane.

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:20.053
We've been up to
maybe 1.2 Gs in a 30° bank turn,

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.921
and that's about as steep
as we're going to go,

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.123
because we don't need
to do anything more than that.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.426
So maybe we overengineered the airplane.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.461
But, you know, to me, when I'm flying it,

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:31.631
that's the kind of stuff I'm
really interested in having underneath me.

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:42.809
We get a lot of head turning,
we get a lot of waves as we go by.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.845
We're trying to preserve
early aviation history.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.881
I've been airborne and I've looked down
and there's another airplane

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.149
flying underneath of me.

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:53.720
And that's just a view that you don't get
from many other airplanes.

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:04.764
Our original airplane,
we call the Brown Bird.

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.733
It first flew in 1982.

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:09.802
It has flown over 5100 passengers
over the years.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.337
Really fun to fly.

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:14.340
But we're going with something now
that's a little bit more advanced,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.310
a little bit easier to move around.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.390
A lot of people that have
come out over the years

00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:33.159
have said, “hey, I'm a pilot,
I've got years of experience.

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.728
“I would really like to fly your airplane”.

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.730
And we don't let other people
fly the airplane.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.966
And it's very difficult
to fly the airplane.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:43.369
We did make an exception once,
about 15 years ago.

00:06:43.436 --> 00:06:46.873
We took a look at his credentials
and we thought, okay, you know what?

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:48.841
We're going to give this
gentleman a chance.

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.778
And it was impressive
how well he could control the airplane.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.846
His name happened to be
Neil Armstrong.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.083
So we've told other people,
if you’ve walked on the moon,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.051
we’ll probably let you
come fly our airplane.

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:01.421
But until then, you know,
we're just going to

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.723
take you for a ride
when we take you flying.

